    "evidence_text": "Induction of myelodysplasia by myeloid-derived suppressor cells.\n\nBone progenitor dysfunction induces myelodysplasia and secondary leukemia\n\nDeletion of Sbds in mouse osteoprogenitors induced bone marrow dysfunction with myelodysplasia.\n\nLandscape of genetic lesions in 944 patients with myelodysplastic syndromes\n\nWe determined the biological and prognostic significance of genetic aberrations in MDS.\n\nMany mutations were associated with higher risk groups and/or blast elevation.\n\nSurvival was investigated in 875 patients.\n\nTheir pathogenesis is complex and likely depends on interplay between aberrant hematopoietic cells and their microenvironment.\n\nThe molecular basis for MDS progression is unknown, but a key element in MDS disease progression is loss of chromosomal material (genomic instability).\n\nThese data suggest treatment strategies that target RAS/RAC pathways and ROS production in human MDS/AML.\n\nThe NLRP3 inflammasome functions as a driver of the myelodysplastic syndrome phenotype.\n\nHowever, the mechanisms of p53 activation and its relevance for the erythroid defect remain elusive.",
